

------------------------------- "Wireshark doo dooo do doo on picoCTF"-------------
a network forensics challenge 
where I had to:

- Analyze a .pcapng packet capture file
- Filter HTTP traffic in Wireshark
- Extract hidden files from HTTP objects
- Decode ROT13 cipher to find the flag

Step 1 — Opened the PCAP file
A .pcapng file is a recording of all network traffic. Like a security camera recording of every packet that went across the network.
Step 2 — Filtered HTTP
HTTP traffic is unencrypted — meaning we can read everything. We filtered to see only HTTP packets out of 987 total.

Step 3 — Export HTTP Objects
Wireshark can extract actual files that were transferred over HTTP. Like pulling attachments out of an email. We found files that were downloaded during that captured session.

Step 4 — Found the suspicious file
%2f = URL encoded / — that's a weird filename which made it stand out. Real attackers sometimes hide data in files with unusual names.

Step 5 — ROT13 Decoding
The content looked like gibberish — gurt svn vf cypbg — but it was ROT13 encoded. ROT13 shifts every letter 13 positions in the alphabet. It's a simple cipher used in CTFs.
  
